    title: 'Top Banyuwangi Culinary Delights to Savor After Your Trek',
    category: 'Culinary',
    date: 'July 15, 2026',
    readTime: '6 Min Read',
    excerpt: 'Worked up an appetite after seeing the golden sunrise? Refuel your energy with authentic Sego Tempong, Rujak Soto, and Osing specialties.',
    image: '/images/pkg-waterfall.png',
    isFeatured: true,
    author: 'Indonesian Food Enthusiasts',
    content: `After conquering the summit of Mount Ijen and returning to the base in the morning, your appetite is guaranteed to peak. Banyuwangi is celebrated for the unique culinary heritage of the indigenous Osing people, characterized by bold spices and fresh ingredients.

## 1. Sego Tempong (Tempong Rice)

The undeniable king of Banyuwangi street food. The word *"Tempong"* in the local Osing dialect translates to *"slap"*, representing the punchy kick of freshly ground tomato chili paste (*sambal terasi dadakan*).
- **Side Dishes**: Crispy salted fish, fried tofu and tempeh, corn fritters, and boiled seasonal greens such as sweet potato leaves and chayote.
- **Recommended Spots**: Sego Tempong Mbok Wah or Sego Tempong Mbok Nah in central Banyuwangi.

## 2. Rujak Soto

An intriguing combination that sounds unusual but tastes extraordinary: fresh boiled vegetables dressed in savory peanut and petis paste, submerged in piping hot, aromatic beef broth. It is the ultimate comfort food to warm up after the frosty mountain winds.

## 3. Pecel Rawon

Another signature East Javanese mashup: sweet and savory peanut-dressed vegetable salad served atop warm rice, drenched in rich black beef soup brewed with fermented black nuts (*keluak*). Typically accompanied by crispy beef jerky (*empal*) and crunchy peanut brittle.`
