    title: 'How to Layer Your Clothing for the Mount Ijen Climate',
    category: 'Guides',
    date: 'July 2, 2026',
    readTime: '3 Min Read',
    excerpt: 'Temperatures at the crater rim can drop to 8°C, yet the steep climb can make you sweat profusely. Here is the secret to a functional 3-layer system.',
    image: '/images/pkg-bali.png',
    isFeatured: false,
    author: 'Outdoor Gear Specialists',
    content: `A common mistake among first-time trekkers at Mount Ijen is wearing a heavy down parka from the very start, only to overheat and soak their inner layers in sweat midway up the hill.

## The Secret: The 3-Layer System

The key to mountain comfort in tropical volcanic highlands is a flexible **3-layering system**:

### Layer 1: Base Layer (Moisture Wicking)
Wear a quick-dry athletic tee (polyester or merino wool). Avoid heavy cotton shirts, as cotton absorbs sweat, stays damp, and chills you rapidly once you stop moving.

### Layer 2: Mid Layer (Thermal Insulation)
A lightweight fleece jacket or warm sweater. You can easily unbutton or pack this into your daypack while ascending, then put it back on when resting at the windy summit.

### Layer 3: Outer Layer (Wind & Dust Protection)
A breathable windbreaker or shell jacket to block the biting wind gusts on the crater rim and shield your clothes from airborne sulfur dust.

## Handy Accessories
- Lightweight knit gloves to keep fingers warm in the pre-dawn chill.
- Beanie cap to protect your ears from relentless ridge breezes.`
