    slug: "leave-no-trace-cultural-guidelines-mount-ijen",
    title: "Leave No Trace & Cultural Respect at Mount Ijen: Sustainable Tourism Guidelines",
    category: "Local Culture",
    date: "October 25, 2026",
    readTime: "4 Min Read",
    excerpt: "Help preserve Mount Ijen's UNESCO Global Geopark environment. Key Leave No Trace rules on waste management, wildlife, and respectful tourism.",
    image: "/images/pkg-waterfall.png",
    isFeatured: false,
    author: "Ijen Expedition Team",
    content: "Mount Ijen was officially recognized by UNESCO as part of the **Ijen UNESCO Global Geopark** in 2023. Preserving its fragile volcanic flora, unique endemic bird species, and sacred cultural significance for future generations requires the cooperation of every visitor.\n\n---\n\n## The 5 Core Principles for Mount Ijen\n\n1. **Pack It In, Pack It Out (Bawa Sampahmu Turun)**:\n   - There are no trash collection bins along the mountain trail or inside the crater.\n   - Carry a small trash bag in your daypack for energy snack wrappers, used tissues, and plastic bottle caps. Bring every piece of waste back down to Paltuding.\n2. **Never Discard Wet Wipes in Nature**:\n   - Synthetic wet wipes do not biodegrade and can take decades to break down in sub-alpine soil. Never bury them under rocks or bushes.\n3. **Strict Zero-Smoking Policy**:\n   - Sub-alpine shrubs, mountain edelweiss (*Anaphalis javanica*), and dry savanna grasses are extremely combustible during the dry season. Smoking along the trail is strictly forbidden by BBKSDA rangers.\n4. **Stay on the Main Track**:\n   - Cutting switchbacks creates rapid scree erosion that destroys trails and causes rockfalls onto hikers below.\n5. **Respect Sacred Volcanic Heritage**:\n   - The crater lake is held sacred in local Osing ceremonies (*Petik Laut Kawah Ijen*). Refrain from loud shouting or profane language while inside the crater sanctuary."
